[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: web_server_1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the HTTPS service on the web1 application server (Apache on web_server_1) is not running/listening. Mechanism: modern browsers commonly try HTTPS first (HTTPS-First/HSTS policies). Because web1.local does not accept connections on TCP/443, the browser’s initial attempt stalls/fails, presenting as a page “timing out,” even though plain HTTP on port 80 is healthy.

Localization and evidence:
- Name resolution is correct: host_1_1_1_1 resolves web1.local to 10.200.0.4 (getent hosts).
- HTTP works: curl from host_1_1_1_1 to http://web1.local returns 200 OK with normal latency and headers (Apache/2.4.62).
- HTTPS fails: TCP probe to 443 from both host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1 returns EXIT:1 (connection refused/closed).
- On web_server_1, only port 80 is listening: ss/netstat shows tcp LISTEN 0.0.0.0:80 (apache2) and no listener on 443; iptables is ACCEPT all, so not a firewall block.

Root cause: HTTPS is not configured/enabled on web_server_1 (no Apache SSL vhost/certificate), leaving port 443 closed while clients attempt HTTPS first.

Corrective fix: enable HTTPS on web_server_1. In Apache, enable SSL and a 443 vhost: a2enmod ssl; create/provide a certificate/key; a2ensite default-ssl (or equivalent vhost with Listen 443 and SSLEngine on); systemctl reload/restart apache2. Verify nc -vz web1.local 443 succeeds and curl -k https://web1.local returns 200. As a temporary workaround, advise users to explicitly use http://web1.local until 443 is enabled. No reboot of the server is required. 
=== END ===